WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:10.530
ok guys for this problem we're going to
want to generate this compound starting

00:00:10.530 --> 00:00:14.130
from benzene using any any other
compounds you want. so as a hint we're probably

00:00:14.130 --> 00:00:17.890
gonna be using some electrophilic
aromatic substitution so go ahead and

00:00:17.890 --> 00:00:19.370
see if you can build this molecule

00:00:41.540 --> 00:00:47.499
ok so let's take a look at the two substituents that are on the target
molecule so first we have an alkyl group

00:00:47.499 --> 00:00:52.390
we know that we can attach an alkyl
group onto a benzene ring by Friedel

00:00:52.390 --> 00:01:13.450
Crafts alkylation and then we also have
nitration. but what order are we

00:01:13.450 --> 00:01:16.719
gonna do them in? in order to answer this
question we have to understand something

00:01:16.719 --> 00:01:20.810
about the directing abilities of these
groups. now we know that an alkyl group

00:01:20.810 --> 00:01:27.090
is an ortho/para director and we know
that a nitro group is a meta director

00:01:27.090 --> 00:01:33.009
and we also understand that this spatial
relationship between them on the

00:01:33.009 --> 00:01:37.799
molecule is ortho, so this is gonna tell us
what order we have to do the reactions

00:01:37.799 --> 00:01:43.329
in because given that they have an ortho
relationship to one another it must be

00:01:43.329 --> 00:01:48.960
the ortho/para director that directed
the other group to its position and so

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:52.469
what we're saying is that we must
starting with benzene have done the

00:01:52.469 --> 00:01:58.609
Friedel Crafts alkylation here with this alkyl chloride to get this

00:01:58.609 --> 00:02:05.090
intermediate because if we have this
intermediate then upon nitration we will

00:02:05.090 --> 00:02:07.590
get a mixture of ortho and para

00:02:07.590 --> 00:02:13.040
nitrated products whereas if we were to
do the nitration first if we did the

00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:18.750
nitration first and let's say this was
our nitrated product then if we went

00:02:18.750 --> 00:02:28.450
and did the alkylation we would get meta
direction from the nitro group and so

00:02:28.450 --> 00:02:34.010
the nitro group cannot have been the
compound that was added first because that

00:02:34.010 --> 00:02:37.709
will not give us a structure that
matches our target molecule, instead

00:02:37.709 --> 00:02:43.390
we're recognizing that alkyl is ortho/para, nitro group is meta, so it must

00:02:43.390 --> 00:02:48.599
have been the alkyl group that directed
the nitro group to the ortho position, we

00:02:48.599 --> 00:02:53.079
can get this mixture, separate them out
and there is going to be our target molecule

